{"author":{"id":"417295a7cc0952a9a38b67be3a894daed91f9069f7407e038d12a4f9b9a8451e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792365555,"nonce":"rtnvbblPzQ09d3aRLSuojNrxb0A=","title":"Debugger: breakpoints, stepping, frames, variables and evaluate, in the VM and the host protocol","message":"RayDE's Extension Studio needs to debug a script as it is written (RayDE issue 91ad7c0). The VM and the host now support it.\n\nVM (crates/rayscript-vm/src/vm/debug.rs): breakpoints by file and line (a line with no code moves to the next one that has), pause, continue, step over / in / out, the stopped fiber's frames (function, file, line, col), the variables in scope at a frame (Proto.locals records each named local's register, cell flag and instruction range), the module's globals, expansion of arrays, objects, maps and signals, and evaluation of a path (name, members, indexes; no arithmetic or calls). A fiber stops before an instruction that has not run; while one is stopped the VM runs nothing and view_step does not flush.\n\nMechanism: a new Op::Break replaces the instruction at each stop, and the original is kept; a hit that does not stop the fiber puts the original back and the next poll puts the Break back. The interpreter has no per-instruction test. First attempt, a flag tested before each instruction: 35%% slower on a tight loop; a copy of the interpreter generic over a debugging const: about 5%% slower; Op::Break: no measurable difference (min user time of 8 alternating runs of a 40M-iteration loop, 3.63 s against 3.63 s). Stepping puts Breaks on the line starts of the stopped fiber's frames, and on the first instruction of every function for step into; a pause asked for puts one on every instruction.\n\nHost (rayscript-host, protocol documented in the crate docs): debug breakpoints / pause / continue / stepOver / stepIn / stepOut / variables / expand / evaluate, and paused / resumed / breakpoints / variables / children / evaluated back. While stopped the host holds events and answers the debug messages; a pause stops a script that never ends, which the process test checks over real pipes.\n\nLimits: a call a built-in makes back into script (array.map(f)) cannot be stopped in; no stop on an error or a debugger statement yet; evaluate takes paths only; upvalues are not named, so a captured variable of an enclosing function shows only when it is a cell the frame holds.","files":null}]}